---
/** Track validity guide — how GlideComp checks that a submitted tracklog is
 * plausibly the pilot's flight of that task, which checks withhold a track
 * from scoring, and how a scorekeeper overrules the automatic verdict.
 * Sibling of the GAP, open-distance and data-cleaning pages.
 *
 * The thresholds quoted here are the constants in engine track-quality.ts —
 * keep them in step. */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import BackLink from "../../components/BackLink.astro";
import SpecRef from "../../components/SpecRef.astro";

const S7A_PDF =
  "https://www.fai.org/sites/default/files/civl/documents/sporting_code_s7_a_-_cross_country_2025.pdf";
---

<Base
  title="GlideComp - How Track Validity Checks Work"
  description="How GlideComp checks that a tracklog is really that pilot's flight of that task — which checks withhold a track from scoring, which only flag it, and how a scorekeeper overrules the verdict."
>
  <div class="min-h-screen bg-background text-foreground py-6 px-page-safe">
    <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      <header class="mb-10">
        <BackLink href="/scoring" label="Scoring" />
        <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold flex items-center gap-3">
          <img src="/icon.svg" alt="GlideComp logo" class="w-8 h-8" />
          How Track Validity Checks Work
        </h1>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-1">
          Checking that a tracklog is really your flight of this task
        </p>
      </header>

      {/* Table of Contents */}
      <nav class="mb-10 p-4 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card">
        <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground mb-2">
          Contents
        </h2>
        <ul class="space-y-1 text-sm">
          <li><a href="#why" class="hover:underline">Why Tracks Get Checked</a></li>
          <li><a href="#withheld" class="hover:underline">The Two Checks That Withhold a Track</a></li>
          <li><a href="#flags" class="hover:underline">The Three Flags That Never Change a Score</a></li>
          <li><a href="#score" class="hover:underline">What It Does to the Score</a></li>
          <li><a href="#override" class="hover:underline">The Scorekeeper Decides</a></li>
          <li><a href="#calibration" class="hover:underline">Where the Thresholds Come From</a></li>
          <li><a href="#disclosure" class="hover:underline">Nothing Happens Silently</a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>

      {/* Why */}
      <section id="why" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Why Tracks Get Checked</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Sometimes a pilot uploads the wrong file, such as a flight from another day or
            another site. It's an easy mistake to make and a hard one to spot: the file is valid
            IGC, it opens, and it looks like a flight.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Scoring alone won't catch it. Because tasks often run across UTC midnight, GlideComp
            works out start gates and deadlines <em>from the flight being scored</em>, not from a
            fixed date. So a tracklog from another day simply moves the whole task onto its own
            day and scores as if nothing were wrong.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            <a href={S7A_PDF} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline hover:text-foreground">FAI S7A §4.4.2</a>
            makes catching this the software's job: the points that verify a flight must have
            "occurred at reasonable times (e.g. on the day in question)".
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Withheld */}
      <section id="withheld" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">The Two Checks That Withhold a Track</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Both checks ask one question: <strong class="text-foreground">is this file this
            flight?</strong> Both use margins wide enough that no honest track comes near them.
          </p>

          <div class="p-4 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card">
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-1">Wrong day</h3>
            <p class="text-muted-foreground">
              GlideComp compares the fixes with the task's calendar day, in the competition's time
              zone, padded by four hours at each end. The check fails only when
              <strong class="text-foreground">every</strong> fix falls outside that window, so an
              overnight recorder, a dawn launch and a flight across local midnight all pass. A
              track up to one day further out is only flagged, not withheld, because a recorder
              with its date set a day wrong is a common fault on a genuine flight.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-4 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card">
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-1">Wrong location</h3>
            <p class="text-muted-foreground">
              No fix comes within <strong class="text-foreground">100 km</strong> of any
              turnpoint. A long course doesn't need a bigger margin: the turnpoints are spread
              along it, and every pilot who launched starts within metres of one.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            A track that fails either check is left out of scoring, and out of the comp and task
            analysis, where its clock and its air would skew the day's statistics for everyone
            else.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Flags */}
      <section id="flags" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">The Three Flags That Never Change a Score</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            These flags describe the flying, not the file. Each can also fire on a genuine
            flight, so GlideComp shows them for a person to judge. They
            <strong class="text-foreground">never change a score</strong>.
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-3 text-muted-foreground">
            <li>
              <strong class="text-foreground">Never left the take-off cylinder.</strong> This is
              what a pilot who launches and sinks straight back down looks like, and they rightly
              get the minimum distance. It is also what a recorder left running on launch looks
              like.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-foreground">No sign of flight.</strong> There is no sustained
              climb (1.5 m/s for two minutes) and no sustained glide (90 seconds of descent at
              0.8 m/s, never slower than 4 m/s over the ground, and roughly straight). This
              catches a vario that recorded the drive up to launch, which speed alone can't,
              because a retrieve vehicle is faster than a paraglider.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-foreground">Impossible speed.</strong> Ground speed stays above
              about 119 km/h for paragliders, or 162 km/h for hang gliders, for a full minute,
              tailwind included. The one-minute window filters out GPS jitter. This flag looks
              for something that isn't a glider.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Score */}
      <section id="score" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">What It Does to the Score</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            A pilot who launches and lands short still gets the
            <a href="/scoring/gap#minimum-distance" class="underline hover:text-foreground">minimum distance</a>,
            usually 5 km. That doesn't change: bad luck on a short flight is still a flight.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            A withheld tracklog is different, because it isn't evidence that the pilot flew this
            task. Those pilots stay in the scores, placed last with
            <strong class="text-foreground">zero points</strong> and the reasons beside their
            name. GlideComp never removes them.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            No other pilot's score moves. The withheld pilot is left out of the launch-validity
            count (<SpecRef section="10.1" label="S7F §10.1" />) rather than counted as having
            flown, so a wrong verdict can only ever cost that one pilot.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Override */}
      <section id="override" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">The Scorekeeper Decides</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Under
            <a href={S7A_PDF} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline hover:text-foreground">S7A §4.4.6</a>,
            rejecting a tracklog is the
            <strong class="text-foreground">organiser's</strong> call, not the software's. A
            scorekeeper can accept a flagged tracklog in one step. It is then scored normally,
            its findings stay visible, and the competition's public audit log records the
            decision.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            If your track was withheld, upload the right file. If the file you uploaded
            <em>is</em> the right one, talk to the scorekeeper.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Calibration */}
      <section id="calibration" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Where the Thresholds Come From</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Wrongly zeroing a pilot is worse than letting a bad file through, so the thresholds
            are set from real competition data.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Across <strong class="text-foreground">4,762</strong> archived tracks, the typical
            flight passes within 70 m of a turnpoint. The furthest any honest track stayed from
            every turnpoint was 45 km. The 100 km threshold is more than twice that.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            The one-day grace comes from the same data. In one archived task, three of eighty
            pilots had recorders stamped a day late. All three flew the task and were scored
            normally in the published results; one finished fourth, in goal. A rule that rejected
            a one-day offset would have given that pilot zero.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Across all <strong class="text-foreground">5,112</strong> tracks GlideComp holds, the
            checks withhold exactly one: a New Zealand flight submitted to a Victorian task ten
            days later.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Disclosure */}
      <section id="disclosure" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Nothing Happens Silently</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            When a check fires, the pilot's report card names it, shows the numbers behind it,
            and says what to do next. The scores table also labels a withheld track, so a zero
            never looks like a glitch.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            It's the same rule as
            <a href="/scoring/data-cleaning" class="underline hover:text-foreground">track data cleaning</a>:
            everything GlideComp did on the way to a score must be visible.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
